<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Noricum</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 16 BCE (approx.) · capital Noreia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Celtic kingdom in the eastern Alps, famed for its iron and steel, that became a Roman ally and then a Roman province without a major war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Noricum grew out of a union of Celtic tribes in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norici">Norici</a>, during the 2nd century BCE. Its core lay in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a> and a centre the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noreia">Noreia</a>. In 113 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defended it against the migrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a>, who defeated a Roman army at Noreia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom was ruled by kings over a federation of tribes and was known across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> for ferrum Noricum, a high-quality <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> produced from local ores. Italian merchants settled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a> trading post, and Noricum minted its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins. Close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> made it a client kingdom; its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voccio">Voccio</a> sent cavalry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 49 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 16 BCE Noricum was absorbed peacefully into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, becoming the province of Noricum with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virunum">Virunum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celje">Celeia</a>. Roman rule lasted until the late 5th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-saint-severinus">Life of Saint Severinus</a> records the last Roman communities before their evacuation in 488.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-noricum.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Noricum" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Noricum_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 16 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Noreia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Voccio, Cincibilus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism, later Roman religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Noric (Celtic), Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regnum Noricum, Noricum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-108&amp;focus=kingdom-of-noricum" aria-label="Kingdom of Noricum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 536 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Noricum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 792L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 792h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 792h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-108&amp;focus=kingdom-of-noricum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

